# The deactivate is the one step ahead of a forced member's own build,
# and it can fail: here gegl's own build broke, so it was never
# installed in the guest, and `port -f deactivate gegl` throws before
# gegl-devel is even linted. gegl-devel does not require gegl — it
# conflicts with it — so the runner reaches it (D25: the loop goes on
# past a failure and skips only what depended on it) and takes the same
# failed path it takes for any step — member=no, the state file says
# failed, and the loop goes on — so the failure is gegl-devel's own,
# read from its own section in MacPorts' own words, and the judge trusts
# the state file (D25) rather than re-deriving it.
#
# The blame reader does not blame gegl: "port deactivate failed: …" is
# not the "Failed to <phase> gegl:" shape a dependency failure wears, so
# nobody is named and the member owns its failure. Its run still carries
# the sibling it was told to deactivate, because that was recorded at
# submit whatever the guest then did with it.
# SYNTHESIZED from the cohort runner in internal/verify/tart/tart.go,
# not captured: see README.md.
members: libwidget gegl gegl-devel
outcome: failed
libwidget.state: passed
libwidget.lint: clean
libwidget.reported: passed
gegl.state: failed
gegl.detail: Failed to build gegl: command execution failed
gegl.reported: failed
gegl-devel.state: failed
gegl-devel.detail: port deactivate failed: Image error: port gegl is not active.
gegl-devel.reported: failed
gegl-devel.forced: gegl
